---
title: "Convert REST JSON to QQL"
description: "Capture live Qdrant REST traffic with qql record and rewrite it as QQL with qql convert — same requests, same results, no hand translation."
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

Existing Qdrant applications speak REST JSON. QQL can read that JSON and
produce the equivalent QQL statements, so a migration or an onboarding step
does not start with a rewrite by hand.

Two commands do the work:

- **`qql record`** captures live traffic from any SDK, dashboard, or script
  by proxying it — the application keeps sending the same requests.
- **`qql convert`** turns one captured request, or a bare Qdrant REST body,
  into canonical QQL.

## Capture live traffic (`qql record`)

`qql record` is a transparent proxy. Point an application at its listen
address instead of Qdrant and change nothing else; every request is forwarded
to the real server unchanged, and each bodied `/collections/` request is
appended to a JSONL capture.

`qql record` is opt-in because it pulls in the proxy runtime:

```bash
cargo build --release -p qql-cli --features record
```

{% terminal title="Record once, convert after" %}
# Qdrant stays where it is (default :6333); the app moves to :6334.
qql record --listen 127.0.0.1:6334 --target http://127.0.0.1:6333 \
  --out capture.jsonl --qql-out capture.qql

# Then, with no recorder running:
qql convert --collection docs capture.jsonl
{% /terminal %}

With `--qql-out`, each captured request is converted as it arrives, so
`capture.qql` is ready when the recording stops. Requests that cannot be
converted are written as `# ERROR` annotations and never interrupt
forwarding.

Defaults are chosen so a bare `qql record` works with a local Qdrant: Qdrant
keeps serving on `127.0.0.1:6333`, and the recorder listens on
`127.0.0.1:6334` so the application only needs its base URL changed:

```bash
qql record
```

Query strings are forwarded but not captured: `wait`, `timeout`, and
`consistency` are endpoint features, not QQL statement content. Bodies are
buffered in memory per request, which is fine for interactive captures and
large batched upserts, but this is a development tool, not a production
proxy.

## Convert a request (`qql convert`)

`qql convert` reads a wrapped request, or a bare Qdrant body, from a file or
stdin and prints canonical QQL.

{% terminal title="Convert a search request" %}
qql convert search.json
cat search.json | qql convert
{% /terminal %}

A wrapped request carries the endpoint, so the collection is derived from
the path:

```json
{
  "method": "POST",
  "path": "/collections/docs/points/query",
  "body": {
    "query": { "nearest": [0.1, 0.2] },
    "using": "dense",
    "limit": 5,
    "filter": { "must": [{ "key": "status", "match": { "value": "active" } }] }
  }
}
```

```sql
QUERY [0.1, 0.2]
FROM docs
USING dense
WHERE status = 'active'
LIMIT 5;
```

A bare body has no path to derive the collection from. Pass it explicitly
with `--collection`:

{% terminal title="Bare body with an explicit collection" %}
echo '{"ids": [1, "point-2"]}' | qql convert --collection docs
# QUERY POINTS (1, 'point-2') FROM docs;
{% /terminal %}

Without `--collection`, a bare body renders `FROM unknown` so the omission
is visible rather than guessed.

## What is covered

Conversion is part of the same contract as execution: Qdrant's OpenAPI
schema describes each request, QQL's typed AST is the shared request model,
and QQL's own formatter renders the output. The converter therefore emits
the same statements the runtime plans, including every clause QQL supports.

| Request | QQL |
|---|---|
| `POST .../points/query`, `.../query/groups` | `QUERY` (including formula, fusion, prefetch, and grouped forms) |
| `POST .../points/scroll`, `.../points/count`, `.../facet` | `SCROLL`, `COUNT`, `FACET` |
| `POST .../points`, `.../points/delete` | `QUERY POINTS`, `DELETE` |
| `POST .../points/payload`, `payload/clear`, `payload/delete` | `UPDATE … SET PAYLOAD`, `CLEAR PAYLOAD`, `DELETE PAYLOAD` |
| `PUT .../points/vectors`, `POST .../points/vectors/delete` | `UPDATE … SET VECTOR`, `DELETE VECTOR` |
| `PUT .../points` | `UPSERT` |
| `PUT|PATCH|DELETE /collections/{name}` | `CREATE COLLECTION`, `ALTER COLLECTION`, `DROP COLLECTION` |
| `PUT .../index`, `DELETE .../index/{field}` | `CREATE INDEX`, `DROP INDEX` |
| shard keys, quotas, `SHOW` metadata | `CREATE` / `DROP SHARD KEY`, `SET QUOTA`, `SHOW …` |

Filter conversion is structural: `must` → `AND`, `should` → `OR`,
`must_not` → `NOT`, and `match`, `range`, `geo_bounding_box`,
`geo_radius`, `geo_polygon`, `has_id`, `is_empty`, and `is_null` lower onto
their typed QQL predicates.

## Why the output can be trusted

- **One emitter.** The converter decodes JSON into the typed AST and prints
  it with `qql fmt`'s formatter. It never builds QQL by string
  concatenation, so canonical output always re-parses.
- **Contract tests.** The covered routes are asserted against
  `crates/qql-runtime/openapi.json` at test time, and every converted
  statement is re-planned and compared against the REST route the runtime
  would have produced from the original QQL.
- **Typed failures.** Inputs QQL cannot represent fail with a specific
  error and a field path (`UnsupportedEndpoint`, `UndecodableBody`,
  `InvalidField`, `InvalidJson`) instead of dropping a clause or emitting
  placeholder text.

{% aside type="note" title="Converted output is normal QQL" %}
There is no compatibility mode. The output uses the same statements as any
hand-written QQL, runs with `qql exec` or `qql execute`, and can be fed to
`qql check` for staged triage before executing.
{% /aside %}

## A migration path in practice

1. **Capture** the requests the application actually sends
   (`qql record --out capture.jsonl`).
2. **Convert** them (`qql convert --collection <name> capture.jsonl`) and
   review the file; it is plain QQL.
3. **Check** representative statements (`qql check "<statement>"`).
4. **Move** data with [Cluster migration](/docs/operations/cluster-migration/)
   when the target is a new cluster, or **replay** the script with
   `qql execute`.

Related references: [CLI](/docs/tools/cli/),
[QQL vs raw Qdrant JSON API](/docs/guides/qql-vs-qdrant-json/).
